<HTML>
<HEAD>
<TITLE>HelpHTML: Program to read html documents from DOS sessions
</TITLE>
</HEAD>
<BODY>
<H1><img src="vecras.gif"> HelpHTML: Program to read html documents from DOS sessions
DOS </H1>
<P>
This program is a reduced html file reader that runs under DOS
sessions. It has been initially thought to be called from other 
programs that use it to show help files in an easy way. However,
it can also be used as a general html reader. For example, if 
you want to see an html document taken from the Internet via ftp,
you can see it from a DOS session as with your web explorer.
<P>
The first parameter is the htm file name with path and extension.
You can indicate a particular document seccion with a '#'. For instance:
"help.htm#seccion2".
<P>
The second parameter (optional) is used to call HelpHTML from 
another application, so that it can show the cover in the first
page of the help. In this case the program will call a file with the same 
name than the executable but with the HTM extension. These files 
must be in the same disk and directory. In C programming language 
this can be done through a function like this:
<PRE>
/* Return 0 on success
          1 if there is not enough memory
          2 if no system calls can be done */
#define TITOL_PROG_HELP TITOL_PROG
int mostra_helphtml(const char *nom_exe, int linies_portada)
{
    char *nom_helphtml, *szcomanda;

    if (NULL==(nom_helphtml=strdup(nom_exe)))
        return 1;
    strcpy(nom_helphtml+strlen(nom_helphtml)-4,&quot;.HTM&quot;);
    if (NULL==(szcomanda=malloc(256)))
    {
        free(nom_helphtml);
        return 1;
    }
    sprintf(szcomanda, TITOL_PROG_HELP&quot; %s 0 %d 0&quot;, nom_helphtml, linies_portada);
    free(nom_helphtml);
    if (-1==system(szcomanda))
    {
        free(szcomanda);
        return 2;
    }
    free(szcomanda);
    return 0;
}

</PRE>
To call the previous function you should do:
<PRE>
#define LINIES_PORTADA 6
portada();                    /* The function portada has to be defined  */
if (mostra_helphtml(argv[0],LINIES_PORTADA))
{
    puts(&quot;Error when calling the help of the program.&quot;);
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

</PRE>
<P>
Not all the html commands are supported by the program, but those 
that are not easily supported are ignored.<BR>
This is the current list of supported commands:
<BR>
<PRE>
&lt;pre&gt; and &lt;/pre&gt;   formatted text  (tabulations are not tolerated)
&lt;blockquote&gt; and &lt;/blockquote&gt; indented text 
&lt;title&gt; and &lt;/title&gt;            title
&lt;p&gt;                                   end of paragraph
&lt;br&gt;                                  end of line
&lt;hr&gt;                                  horizontal line
</PRE>
<P>
Also are supported sorted, unsorted and definition lists:<BR>
<PRE>
&lt;ul&gt;   non indexed list
&lt;ol&gt;   indexed list  (only numbers)
&lt;li&gt;   entrance of list
&lt;dl&gt;   list of definitions
&lt;dt&gt;   entrance to define
&lt;dd&gt;   definition of the entrance
&lt;/dl&gt;  end of the definition list

</PRE>
<P>
Listscan only be one level nested
16.
<P>
The following commands are partially supported:
<BR>
<PRE>
&lt;h1&gt;  &lt;h2&gt;  &lt;h3&gt;  &lt;h4&gt;  &lt;h5&gt;  &lt;h6&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;/h2&gt; &lt;/h3&gt; &lt;/h4&gt; &lt;/h5&gt; &lt;/h6&gt; 
   a change of letter type is equivalent to a change of paragraph.
</PRE>
<P>
It is also supported a reference to a figure:
<PRE>
&lt;img src=&quot;c:/img.gif&quot; alt=&quot;Text alternatiu&quot; &gt;
    In this case the <B>alternative text</B> is shown.
</PRE>
<HR>
<P>
This program supports that the html files contains a section called 
'exclusiva' (exclusive), that will be the only section read if indicated 
in the command line of HelpHTML. This exclusive section is written
in the html file as follows:
<PRE>
&lt;exclusiva>
&lt;/exclusiva>
</PRE>
<P>
This section is normally read by standard html lectors or if you
choose the normal lecture option of the program. It can be used to 
view only the syntax information of the program of a html help file,
neglecting the rest of information in the html file. 
<P>
Also, we define a especial type of text invisible for standard html 
lectors or if you choose the normal lecture option of the program. 
This text only can be visualized if you indicate exclusive mode in the 
command line of HelpHTML program. This text preserves the original 
format and must be included in a especial type of comment:

<PRE>&lt;!--  $$EXCLUSIVA$$
Text only 
visible
in exclusive seccion
--></PRE>

<P>
You can also use it in a program by calling a function in C language 
such as:
<PRE>
int mostra_sintaxihtml(const char *nom_exe, int linies_portada)
{
    char *nom_helphtml, *szcomanda;

    if (NULL==(nom_helphtml=strdup(nom_exe)))
        return 1;
    strcpy(nom_helphtml+strlen(nom_helphtml)-4,&quot;.HTM&quot;);
    if (NULL==(szcomanda=malloc(256)))
    {
        free(nom_helphtml);
        return 1;
    }
    sprintf(szcomanda, TITOL_PROG_HELP&quot; %s 1 %d 0&quot;, nom_helphtml, linies_portada);
    free(nom_helphtml);
    if (-1==system(szcomanda))
    {
        free(szcomanda);
        return 2;
    }
    free(szcomanda);
    return 0;
}
</PRE>
To call the previous function it is required to do the following:
<PRE>
#define LINIES_PORTADA 6
portada();                    /* The portada function has to be defined */
if (mostra_sintaxihtml(argv[0],LINIES_PORTADA))
    puts(&quot; Error when calling the help of the program.&quot;);
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

</PRE>
NOTA: The functions here explained have been recently modified to control more situations. Consult Joan Masó Pau to know the improved syntax.
<HR>
<P>
This is the list of supported special characters:
<PRE>
&amp;lt;        &lt;
&amp;gt;        &gt;
&amp;amp;       &amp;
&amp;quot;      &quot;
&amp;copy;      &copy;
&amp;reg;       &reg;

&amp;Agrave;    &Agrave;
&amp;agrave;    &agrave;
&amp;Aacute;    &Aacute;
&amp;aacute;    &aacute;
&amp;Egrave;    &Egrave;
&amp;egrave;    &egrave;
&amp;Eacute;    &Eacute;
&amp;eacute;    &eacute;
&amp;Iacute;    &Iacute;
&amp;iacute;    &iacute;
&amp;Ograve;    &Ograve;
&amp;ograve;    &ograve;
&amp;Oacute;    &Oacute;
&amp;oacute;    &oacute;
&amp;Uacute;    &Uacute;
&amp;uacute;    &uacute;
&amp;Ccedil;    &Ccedil;
&amp;ccedil;    &ccedil;
&amp;Ntilde;    &Ntilde;
&amp;ntilde;    &ntilde;
&amp;Iuml;      &Iuml;
&amp;iuml;      &iuml;
&amp;Uuml;      &Uuml;
&amp;uuml;      &uuml;

&amp;Acirc;     &Acirc;
&amp;acirc;     &acirc;
&amp;Auml;      &Auml;
&amp;auml;      &auml;
&amp;Aelig;     &Aelig;
&amp;aelig;     &aelig;
&amp;Ouml;      &Ouml;
&amp;ouml;      &ouml;
&amp;Oslash;    &Oslash;
&amp;oslash;    &oslash;
&amp;szlig;     &szlig;
&amp;yuml;      &yuml;   (note the lack of tilde)

</PRE>
<P>
The main gap of this program is that it does not support hypertext.
However, the help can be written in hypertext, and this do not 
affect the program (as any other not supported command, it is 
ignored); nevertheless, any standard navigator would read it.
<BR>
<P>
Limitations =&gt;&gt; Longest command: 5000 characters,
Largest title: 80 characters.
<HR>
<P>
Syntax:<BR>
<EXCLUSIVA> 
<a name="sintaxi"></a>
SYNTAX: HtmlFile [n] [Despl] [p]
<UL>
<LI>HtmlFile is an standard html file.
<LI>[n] 
<UL>
<LI>0 if you want to read all the html file (default value).
<LI>1 if you want to read only the exclusive section.
</UL>
<LI>[Despl] is the number of lines a text written by a previous application
occupy; it is used to skip the previous text on the screen. By default it
is 0.
<LI>[p] Every page has a header and a footer...
<UL>
<LI>1 if you want to see the header of page 1 and the footer 
 of the last (default).
<LI>0 if you do not want to see neither the header of page 1 nor the 
 footer of the last page (option thought for calls from other applications).
</UL>
</UL>
<P>
<!--     $$EXCLUSIVA$$

To obtain the complete help, please type:
HelpHTML ?
-->
</EXCLUSIVA>
</BODY>
